codex
The distinction is now clear: an unchanged value can be a sound oracle when the guest must execute a later read and publish it over a poisoned output slot. I’m checking those execution dependencies row by row. A separate run cannot supply that evidence, and several apparent companion checks turn out to use separate runs.

codex
Two checks that look positive can still pass without progress: a flag extracted from an untouched poison value can already equal the expected bit, and a repeated read can leave the previous correct value in its destination register. I’m including those in the census; excluding them merely because their assertions use equality would repeat the naming mistake you warned about.

codex
The probe comments themselves contain a claim that fails the stricter check: `R13 recount` in `gdrom_cond_probe.py` has a positive dispatch message, but that message precedes the later re-arm access the row is meant to protect. I’m treating evidence before the relevant access as insufficient, even when a comment calls the row protected.

codex
I’ve finished the main pass and am reconciling the row lists with the per-file totals. The largest practical gaps are in interrupt-delivery checks, DMA and device-write refusals, and repeat-access checks that never establish that the suppressed access occurred. The startup and survival controls generally offer less repair value.

